#edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0663d30d-9045-55b3-9c5e-7d0dcf4eda51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543473 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘})+\\frac{3}{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2}-{60}^{∘} & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{3x}{2} & ={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={360}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={600}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={200}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain\n$0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={120}^{∘},$ and $x={200}^{∘}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={200}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543494 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543494 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543494 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44457776-708f-510a-afa3-90718ee8b770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543494 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{120}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{240}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={60}^{∘}$ and $x={120}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={240}^{∘}$ and $x={300}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘},\\,{120}^{∘},\\,{240}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={240}^{∘},$ the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ is $60\\cdot 240=14\\,400.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘})-2=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},$ and ${x}_{6}.$ Determine ${x}_{2},$ ${x}_{4},$ and ${x}_{6}.$\n\n$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{6}=$ \n{{field-3}}${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "468c5cf8-2c4e-5dd6-96eb-d94e2d3f4648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543495 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘})-2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘}) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+{30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+{30}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 3x+{30}^{∘} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={60}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={300}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={270}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={10}^{∘}$ and $x={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={130}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={250}^{∘}$ and $x={330}^{∘}.$\n\nAll other integer values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$  \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={10}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={130}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={210}^{∘},\\,{x}_{5}={250}^{∘},\\,{x}_{6}={330}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543504 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "597abfab-9fa0-5884-9650-9d3a1ca7b418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543504 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={480}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{240}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{480}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={160}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={80}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={320}^{∘}$ and $x={400}^{∘}.$ We reject the solution ${400}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={80}^{∘},\\,{160}^{∘},\\,{320}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}={80}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={320}^{∘},$ the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\text{ }+{x}_{3}$ is $80+320=400.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543509 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+{30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "637ba677-860c-50d3-9633-f9db8d1adb0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543509 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+{30}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}+{30}^{∘} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & ={60}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={300}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & ={270}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={540}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-{660}^{∘}$ and $x=-{180}^{∘}.$  We reject the solution $-{660}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={60}^{∘}$ and $x={540}^{∘}.$  We reject the solution ${540}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={-180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={60}^{∘}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+{20}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6},{x}_{7},$ and ${x}_{8}.$ Determine ${x}_{3},$ ${x}_{6},$ and ${x}_{8}.$\n\n$\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{6}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{8}=$ \n{{field-3}}${}^{∘}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "689ab27d-73f0-5878-911e-f4bfe8b4c90e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543512 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{20}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 4x+{20}^{∘} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={60}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={300}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={40}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={280}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={70}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={10}^{∘}$ and $x={70}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={100}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={190}^{∘}$ and $x={250}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={280}^{∘}$ and $x={340}^{∘}.$\n\nAll other integer values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={10}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{2} & ={70}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{3} & ={100}^{∘}, & {x}_{4} & ={160}^{∘},\\, \\\\ {x}_{5} & ={190}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{6} & ={250}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{7} & ={280}^{∘}, & {x}_{8} & ={340}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-274686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+2=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}$,\\,{x}_{5}=$ \n{{field-5}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7afac99d-b74c-555b-b269-d08cbbf29447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543520 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\pi +2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi +2n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{3}=\\pi ,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{5}=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2=0,$ for $0<x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d85e958-8094-54f0-80e0-53c48caaf572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543527 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{18}+n\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$  We reject the solution $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8471" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543533 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9acc1f61-f727-5595-afa3-5b687cb64df5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543533 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for 