pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n-5)\\pi }{12} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(4n+1)\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We reject $-\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ We reject $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x\\le \\pi .$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{48}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543555 #edb/ref 1069 "q-339149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543555 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543555 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $cos\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}, $\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543555 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf7fd672-fd24-5897-b0f7-1572fe68c96d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543555 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\p